---

<!--
  SERVICE AREA + MAP (2026-08 design reference)

  Replaces the centered 48-city word-cloud, which the reference flagged as
  hard to scan ("viewer might get lost"). The map gives the region at a
  glance; the city list stays as real, crawlable HTML underneath.

  SEO note: every one of the 48 cities is a plain <a> in the markup, not
  injected by script and not hidden behind a click. The <details> element
  collapses the list visually, but its contents are in the DOM and remain
  indexable - crawlers read collapsed <details> content, and it stays
  keyboard- and screen-reader accessible without any JS.
-->
<section class={`${surface} py-20 sm:py-24`} aria-labelledby="service-area-heading">
  <Container>
    <div class="text-center" data-reveal>
      <!-- Heading widened from "The Greater Seattle Area and Beyond":
           Pinky's also operates in Texas (license MP#44935), so a
           Seattle-only heading understated the footprint. The supporting
           line names both markets explicitly and says "locations" for
           Texas rather than anything implying statewide coverage - only
           San Antonio and Corpus Christi are confirmed. -->
      <p class="font-display text-sm font-bold tracking-wider text-pink-600 uppercase">Proudly Serving</p>
      <h2 id="service-area-heading" class="font-display mt-3 text-4xl font-bold text-balance text-teal-900 sm:text-5xl">
        Pinky's Plumbing Service Areas
      </h2>
      <p class="text-ink-600 text-justify-body text-justify-center-last-always mx-auto mt-4 max-w-2xl text-lg">
        Serving homeowners and businesses across <strong class="text-teal-900">King</strong>, <strong
          class="text-teal-900">Snohomish</strong
        >, and <strong class="text-teal-900">Pierce</strong> counties in the Greater Seattle area — plus <strong
          class="text-teal-900">San Antonio</strong
        > and <strong class="text-teal-900">Corpus Christi</strong>, Texas.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Map + marker.
         Inline SVG rather than an <img> or a mapping library: it is ~4kB of
         markup, needs no extra request, no JS, and no hydration, so it
         cannot regress LCP/INP. The pin is positioned in the SVG's own
         coordinate space so it stays locked to the Puget Sound region at
         every size, instead of being absolutely positioned in CSS and
         drifting as the container scales. -->
    <!-- MAP CONTAINER: premium surface - gradient wash, soft shadow,
         thin low-opacity border, large radius. Padding is deliberately
         tight on phones so the map itself keeps maximum width. -->
    <div class="map-panel relative mt-10 overflow-hidden sm:mt-12" data-reveal>
      <!-- viewBox matches the generated geometry's measured bounds
           (x 22.2..977.8, y 8..612) with a small bleed, so the map fills
           the container instead of floating in dead space. Re-measure if
           the state data is regenerated. -->
      <svg
        viewBox="18 4 964 612"
        class="map-svg mx-auto h-auto w-full"
        role="img"
        aria-labelledby="us-map-title us-map-desc"
      >
        <title id="us-map-title">Map of the contiguous United States</title>
        <desc id="us-map-desc">
          Washington State is highlighted in the north-west, marking Pinky's Plumbing's service area across the Greater
          Seattle region - King, Snohomish, and Pierce counties, covering {serviceAreas.length} cities.
        </desc>

        <defs>
          <!-- Vertical wash across the country fill: the faint top-to-bottom
               shift is what stops 49 flat shapes reading as a wireframe. -->
          <linearGradient id="sa-state-fill" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0" y2="1">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="var(--color-teal-500)"></stop>
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="var(--color-teal-700)"></stop>
          </linearGradient>
          <!-- Washington's fill, brighter at the top so the state reads as
               lit from above rather than as a flat pink cut-out. -->
          <linearGradient id="sa-wa-fill" x1="0" y1="0" x2="0.4" y2="1">
            <stop offset="0%" stop-color="var(--color-pink-300)"></stop>
            <stop offset="100%" stop-color="var(--color-pink-350)"></stop>
          </linearGradient>
        </defs>

        <!-- Every state is its own FILLED path. Filling each individually
             (rather than one national silhouette with hairline borders over
             it) is what gives the map its solidity, and it is what makes
             per-state hover possible. Separator strokes use the panel's own
             background tint so states read as butted tiles. -->
        <g class="map-states">
          {
            US_STATES.map(({ n, d }) =>
              n === 'Washington' ? null : <path d={d} class="map-state" fill="url(#sa-state-fill)" />
            )
          }
        </g>

        <!-- ---- CONTEXT LAYER: state abbreviations ----
             Positions are PROJECTED centroids (d3 geoPath.centroid) run
             through the same geoAlbersUsa fit as the state paths, so every
             label sits inside its own state. Six crowded north-eastern
             states (NJ, DE, RI, CT, MA, NH...) could not hold a centred
             label, so a greedy solver moved them to the first
             collision-free candidate position and they get a leader line
             back to the true centroid. DC is omitted entirely - at this
             scale it projects to a 2x3 unit sliver.
             Verified: 0 overlapping label boxes across all 48 states and
             18 reference cities. -->
        <g class="map-state-labels" aria-hidden="true">
          {
            STATE_LABELS.filter((s) => s.lead).map((s) => (
              <line x1={s.x} y1={s.y} x2={s.lx} y2={s.ly} class="map-leader" />
            ))
          }
          {
            /* WA's abbreviation is omitted: the state already carries the
               pink fill, the pin and the "Greater Seattle" callout, so a
               "WA" on top of all that is redundant and crowds the marker.

               `map-state-label-dense` marks the physically small states
               whose labels sit close enough together that they collide once
               the type scales up on phones (measured: MA/CT overlap 10x4px
               at 390, WV/VA 3x3). They are hidden below 640px rather than
               left overlapping - the full city list below is the real
               content, this layer is context. */
            STATE_LABELS.filter((s) => s.ab !== 'WA').map((s) => (
              <text
                x={s.lx}
                y={s.ly}
                text-anchor="middle"
                dominant-baseline="central"
                class={`map-state-label${s.lead ? ' map-state-label-offset' : ''}${
                  ['CT', 'MA', 'WV', 'NH', 'VT', 'MD', 'NJ', 'DE', 'RI'].includes(s.ab) ? ' map-state-label-dense' : ''
                }`}
              >
                {s.ab}
              </text>
            ))
          }
        </g>

        <!-- ---- CONTEXT LAYER: major US cities ----
             GEOGRAPHIC REFERENCE ONLY - these are deliberately NOT styled
             like the service area, because Pinky's does not serve them.
             Teal dot + small low-opacity label, versus the pink dots and
             high-contrast type used for the real service cities below. -->
        <g class="map-ref-cities" aria-hidden="true">
          {
            REF_CITIES.map((c) => (
              <>
                <circle cx={c.x} cy={c.y} r="3.2" class="map-ref-dot" />
                <text
                  x={c.x + c.dx}
                  y={c.y + c.dy}
                  text-anchor={c.side === 'l' ? 'end' : 'start'}
                  dominant-baseline="central"
                  class="map-ref-label"
                >
                  {c.n}
                </text>
              </>
            ))
          }
        </g>

        <!-- Washington last, so its glow and heavier stroke paint OVER the
             neighbouring states' edges rather than under them.
             It is a real <a> so the highlight is keyboard-reachable and
             announced as a link - the interaction is not hover-only, which
             also makes it work on touch. -->
        <a
          href="#service-area-cities"
          class="map-wa-link"
          aria-label={`View all ${serviceAreas.length} cities we serve in the Greater Seattle area`}
        >
          {
            US_STATES.filter((s) => s.n === 'Washington').map(({ d }) => (
              <path d={d} class="map-state-active" fill="url(#sa-wa-fill)" />
            ))
          }

          <!-- Tooltip, shown on hover AND keyboard focus. Drawn inside the
               SVG (rather than as an HTML overlay) so it scales with the map
               and cannot be clipped by the panel's overflow:hidden.
               pointer-events:none so it never blocks the link underneath.
               Copy is derived from the site's real data - the county names
               come from business.counties, the count from serviceAreas. -->
          <g class="map-tooltip" transform="translate(196 26)" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="0" y="0" width={WA_TOOLTIP_W} height="96" rx="14" fill="var(--color-teal-950)" fill-opacity="0.96"
            ></rect>
            <text x="18" y="32" class="font-display" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="#ffffff">
              Greater Seattle Area
            </text>
            <text x="18" y="56" font-size="16" fill="var(--color-pink-300)">
              {waCountiesLine}
            </text>
            <text x="18" y="80" font-size="16" font-weight="600" fill="#ffffff">
              {serviceAreas.length} locations
            </text>
          </g>
        </a>

        <!-- ---- TEXAS: second active market ----
             Deliberately a SOFTER treatment than Washington. WA gets the
             full pink fill + glow because the site lists a broad 48-city
             Greater Seattle service area; Texas has exactly two confirmed
             locations, so the state is only lightly tinted and the actual
             claim is carried by the two city markers below it. Filling
             Texas as strongly as Washington would imply statewide coverage
             that the source data does not support. -->
        <a
          href="#service-regions"
          class="map-tx-link"
          aria-label="Pinky's Plumbing Texas locations: San Antonio and Corpus Christi"
        >
          {
            US_STATES.filter((s) => s.n === 'Texas').map(({ d }) => (
              <path d={d} class="map-state-tx" fill="var(--color-pink-350)" />
            ))
          }

          <!-- Shares WA_TOOLTIP_W so the two tooltips are the same size and
               read as one component rather than two mismatched boxes. That
               width auto-sizes to the longest county line, which is what
               stopped "Pierce Counties" running out of the panel. -->
          <g class="map-tooltip map-tooltip-tx" transform="translate(300 330)" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="0" y="0" width={WA_TOOLTIP_W} height="96" rx="14" fill="var(--color-teal-950)" fill-opacity="0.96"
            ></rect>
            <text x="18" y="32" class="font-display" font-size="20" font-weight="700" fill="#ffffff">
              Texas Locations
            </text>
            <text x="18" y="56" font-size="16" fill="var(--color-pink-300)">San Antonio &#183; Corpus Christi</text>
            <text x="18" y="80" font-size="16" font-weight="600" fill="#ffffff">24-Hour Emergency Service</text>
          </g>
        </a>

        <!-- The two confirmed Texas locations. Same pink marker language as
             the Washington service dots, so a user reads them as "Pinky's is
             here" - and critically NOT the same as the muted teal reference
             dots used for Dallas/Houston, which Pinky's does not serve. -->
        <g class="map-tx-dots">
          {
            TX_CITIES.map((c) => (
              <>
                <circle cx={c.x} cy={c.y} r="12" class="map-pulse map-tx-pulse" fill="var(--color-pink-500)" />
                <circle cx={c.x} cy={c.y} r="4.6" class="map-tx-dot" />
                {/* San Antonio's label sits to the LEFT of its dot: on the
                    right it collided with the "Houston" reference label
                    (measured 14x5px overlap at 1440). Corpus Christi has
                    clear space to its right, so it keeps the default side. */}
                <text
                  x={c.n === 'San Antonio' ? c.x - 9 : c.x + 9}
                  y={c.y + 1}
                  text-anchor={c.n === 'San Antonio' ? 'end' : 'start'}
                  dominant-baseline="middle"
                  class="map-tx-label"
                >
                  {c.n}
                </text>
              </>
            ))
          }
        </g>

        <!-- ---- SERVICE LAYER: real Pinky's locations ----
             Pink dots, drawn BEFORE the Seattle pin so the pin sits on top.
             Every one of these is validated against the serviceAreas data
             at generation time - nothing here is invented. Their lon/lat
             run through the same projection, so Bellevue really is east of
             Seattle and Tacoma really is south. Hidden below 1024px, where
             Washington is only ~30 units wide and they would merge into a
             single blob. -->
        <g class="map-svc-dots" aria-hidden="true">
          {SERVICE_CITIES.map((c) => <circle cx={c.x} cy={c.y} r="2.6" class="map-svc-dot" />)}
        </g>

        <!-- Seattle marker. Pin, ripple and label are one group so they
             translate together to the projected coordinate. Two offset
             ripples read as a continuous radar sweep rather than a blink. -->
        <g class="map-marker" transform={`translate(${SEATTLE[0]} ${SEATTLE[1]})`}>
          <circle r="16" fill="var(--color-pink-500)" class="map-pulse"></circle>
          <circle r="16" fill="var(--color-pink-500)" class="map-pulse map-pulse-delay"></circle>
          <path
            d="M0 0 C -7 -9 -12 -15 -12 -21 C -12 -28 -6 -33 0 -33 C 6 -33 12 -28 12 -21 C 12 -15 7 -9 0 0 Z"
            fill="var(--color-teal-950)"
            stroke="white"
            stroke-width="2"
            stroke-linejoin="round"></path>
          <circle cy="-21" r="4.5" fill="var(--color-pink-350)"></circle>
        </g>

        <!-- Labels sit BELOW Washington rather than over it (the text is
             wider than the state at this projection) and are LEFT-anchored
             from the state's west edge rather than centred on the pin.
             Centring on the pin at x=102 ran "Greater Seattle" off the
             viewBox's left edge, since the label is ~200 units wide and the
             pin is only ~85 units from the edge. -->
        <!-- Compact on desktop: the inset panel now carries the full
             "Greater Seattle / counties / 48 locations" story, so the
             national map only needs to name the region. The previous
             two-line callout at font-size 27 spanned x 62..258 and printed
             straight over the OR and ID abbreviations (verified by
             measuring both label boxes in user space).
             The sub-label is kept in the markup but hidden at >=1024px,
             because below that breakpoint the inset is hidden and this
             becomes the only place the location count appears. -->
        <!-- Leader from the callout up to the Seattle pin: the label had to
             move clear of the OR/ID abbreviations, and at that distance the
             association with the pink state needs to be drawn rather than
             implied. -->
        <path
          d={`M40 ${186} L40 ${SEATTLE[1] + 22} L${SEATTLE[0] - 6} ${SEATTLE[1] + 10}`}
          class="map-callout-leader"
          fill="none"></path>
        <text
          x="26"
          y="196"
          text-anchor="start"
          class="map-label font-display"
          font-size="20"
          font-weight="700"
          fill="var(--color-teal-950)">Greater Seattle</text
        >
        <text
          x="26"
          y="217"
          text-anchor="start"
          class="map-sublabel font-display"
          font-size="16"
          font-weight="600"
          fill="var(--color-teal-700)">{serviceAreas.length} service locations</text
        >
      </svg>

      <!-- ---- PUGET SOUND INSET ----
           The national map gives geographic context; this gives the actual
           service territory at a scale where all 48 cities are legible.
           Rendered as a sibling panel rather than an overlay so it can
           reflow below the US map on narrow screens instead of covering
           it. Hidden below 1024px - at tablet width the two maps side by
           side would leave neither readable, and the crawlable city list
           below already carries every name. -->
      <div class="map-inset hidden lg:block">
        <svg
          viewBox={`0 0 ${INSET.IW} ${INSET.IH}`}
          class="inset-svg"
          role="img"
          aria-labelledby="inset-title inset-desc"
        >
          <title id="inset-title">Detail map of the Greater Seattle service area</title>
          <desc id="inset-desc">
            King, Snohomish, and Pierce counties in western Washington, showing all {serviceAreas.length} cities served by
            Pinky's Plumbing.
          </desc>

          <!-- Washington's coastline behind the counties, so Puget Sound
               and the shoreline read correctly rather than the counties
               floating on blank space. -->
          <path d={INSET.wa} class="inset-wa"></path>

          <!-- The three real service counties. -->
          {INSET.counties.map((c) => <path d={c.d} class="inset-county" />)}

          <!-- All 48 service cities. Dots first, then labels, so no dot is
               ever drawn over a label. -->
          {INSET.dots.map((c) => <circle cx={c.x} cy={c.y} r="3.1" class="inset-dot" />)}
          {
            INSET.labels.map((c) => (
              <text x={c.x + c.dx} y={c.y + c.dy} dominant-baseline="hanging" class="inset-label">
                {c.n}
              </text>
            ))
          }

          <!-- Seattle emphasised above the rest of the service cities. -->
          {
            INSET.dots
              .filter((c) => c.n === 'Seattle')
              .map((c) => (
                <>
                  <circle cx={c.x} cy={c.y} r="14" class="map-pulse inset-pulse" fill="var(--color-pink-500)" />
                  <circle cx={c.x} cy={c.y} r="5.4" class="inset-dot-hero" />
                </>
              ))
          }
        </svg>

        <p class="inset-caption">
          <span class="inset-caption-title">Greater Seattle</span>
          <!-- Real "·" characters, not &middot; entities: this is a JSX
               expression context, so an HTML entity inside the joined
               string is emitted as literal text. -->
          <span class="inset-caption-sub">
            {business.counties.map((c) => c.name.replace(/ County$/, '')).join(' · ')} Counties · {serviceAreas.length} locations
          </span>
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- "Locations available" chip. A real link to the city list, so the
           badge is useful rather than decorative. -->
      <a
        href="#service-area-cities"
        class="map-chip glass-light shadow-card absolute bottom-5 left-5 hidden items-center gap-2 rounded-full px-4 py-2.5 text-sm font-semibold text-teal-900 transition-all sm:inline-flex lg:top-7 lg:bottom-auto lg:left-auto"
      >
        <MapPin className="size-4 text-pink-600" aria-hidden="true" />
        {serviceAreas.length} locations available
      </a>

      <!-- Minimal legend. Makes the pink/teal distinction explicit so the
           reference cities can never be misread as places Pinky's serves -
           which matters legally as much as visually. Desktop only: on
           phones the contextual city dots are hidden anyway, so there is
           nothing left to disambiguate. -->
      <!-- Bottom-LEFT-of-centre, not bottom-right: the inset card occupies
           the right column from 1024px up. -->
      <div class="map-legend absolute bottom-5 left-5 hidden lg:block" aria-hidden="true">
        <!-- Three tiers, because there are now three distinct things on the
