             The two Pinky's entries share the pink hue so they read as
             related; the reference entry is teal so Dallas/Houston can
             never be mistaken for service locations. -->
        <div class="glass-light shadow-card rounded-2xl px-3.5 py-2.5">
          <p class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-semibold text-teal-900">
            <span class="bg-pink-350/45 inline-block size-2.5 rounded-[3px] ring-1 ring-pink-600/70"></span>
            Pinky's service region
          </p>
          <p class="mt-1.5 flex items-center gap-2 text-xs font-semibold text-teal-900">
            <span class="inline-block size-2.5 rounded-full bg-pink-600 ring-2 ring-white"></span>
            Pinky's location
          </p>
          <p class="text-ink-500 mt-1.5 flex items-center gap-2 text-xs">
            <span class="inline-block size-2.5 rounded-full bg-teal-600/55"></span>
            Geographic reference
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ---- SERVICE REGION CARDS ----
         Both of Pinky's markets, side by side, at EVERY breakpoint. These
         sit outside the map panel (the Puget Sound inset is lg-only) so
         mobile users still get both regions - on a phone these cards are
         the primary way the two markets are communicated, since the in-map
         labels are too small to carry it.

         The Texas card deliberately says "Texas Locations", never
         "Serving Texas": only San Antonio and Corpus Christi are confirmed
         on Pinky's official Texas site, and a statewide phrasing would
         overclaim. The Washington card's counts come from live data
         (business.counties, serviceAreas.length) so they cannot drift. -->
    <div id="service-regions" class="region-cards" data-reveal>
      <div class="region-card">
        <p class="region-card-eyebrow">Washington</p>
        <p class="region-card-title">Greater Seattle</p>
        <p class="region-card-sub">
          {business.counties.map((c) => c.name.replace(/ County$/, '')).join(' · ')} Counties
        </p>
        <a href="#service-area-cities" class="region-card-link">{serviceAreas.length} service locations</a>
      </div>

      <div class="region-card">
        <p class="region-card-eyebrow">Texas</p>
        <p class="region-card-title">Texas Locations</p>
        <p class="region-card-sub">
          {TX_CITIES.map((c) => c.n).join(' · ')}
        </p>
        <p class="region-card-note">24-Hour Emergency Service</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Crawlable city list, collapsed to a short preview.
         Showing all 48 names at once made the section a wall of text that was
         hard to scan (worst on phones, where it ran ~9 rows deep). Only the
         first PREVIEW_COUNT are visible up front; the rest are revealed by the
         "Read more" toggle.

         The collapse is MOBILE-ONLY - see the media-queried rules in the
         styles below. Desktop keeps all 48 visible (they fit in three tidy
         rows there) and drops the toggle entirely.

         SEO: every one of the 48 is a plain <a> in the server-rendered HTML,
         never removed and never injected by script. The overflow items are
         hidden only by a CSS class under a max-width media query, so crawlers
         see the full list and it also degrades to "all cities visible" with
         JS off. -->
    <details id="service-area-cities" class="group mt-10" open>
      <summary
        class="font-display flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-center gap-2 text-base font-bold text-teal-900"
      >
        <MapPin className="size-4 text-pink-600" aria-hidden="true" />
        All {serviceAreas.length} cities we serve
        <span class="text-ink-400 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
      </summary>

      <!-- Sized for 3 rows on desktop. Measured: the 48 names total ~2879px
           of text, so three rows need ~1384px of usable width including
           gaps - more than the 1280px Container allows. `w-screen` with a
           centring translate lets just this list break out of the container
           (capped at 1400px so it never touches the viewport edges), which
           is what gets it to 3 rows instead of 4. max-w-4xl (896px) was the
           original constraint forcing 5. Reverts to normal flow below
           1440px, where it wraps naturally. -->
      <ul
        id="service-area-city-list"
        class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2.5 xl:relative xl:left-1/2 xl:w-[min(1400px,94vw)] xl:-translate-x-1/2"
      >
        {
          orderedAreas.map((city, i) => {
            const slug = featuredSlugs.get(city);
            return (
              <li class:list={['city-item', i >= PREVIEW_COUNT && 'city-item--overflow']}>
                <a
                  href={slug ? `/service-area/${slug}/` : '/service-area/'}
                  class="text-ink-600 text-sm transition-colors hover:text-pink-600"
                >
                  {city}
                </a>
              </li>
            );
          })
        }
      </ul>

      <div class="mt-5 text-center">
        <button
          type="button"
          id="service-area-cities-toggle"
          class="font-display inline-flex cursor-pointer items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full text-sm font-bold text-pink-600 transition-colors hover:text-pink-700 focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-pink-400"
          aria-expanded="false"
          aria-controls="service-area-city-list"
        >
          <span data-label>Read more</span>
          <span class="text-xs transition-transform" data-chevron aria-hidden="true">▾</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </details>

    <!-- "Don't see your area?" CTA, per the reference. -->
    <div class="mt-14 text-center" data-reveal>
      <h3 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold text-teal-900">Don't see your area?</h3>
      <p class="text-ink-600 mt-2">Contact us to find out whether we serve your neighborhood.</p>
      <!-- Same 2-up equal-size grid as the SplitFeature CTA pair, so the two
           buttons match each other instead of sizing to their label lengths
           (measured 172px vs 257px when left to flex-wrap). -->
      <div class="mx-auto mt-6 grid w-full max-w-sm grid-cols-2 items-stretch gap-3.5 lg:max-w-lg">
        <Button href="/contact-us/" variant="primary" size="lg" icon={ArrowRight} class="area-cta">Contact Us</Button>
        <Button href="/service-area/" variant="secondary" size="lg" icon={ArrowRight} class="area-cta">
          <span class="sm:hidden">Service Area</span>
          <span class="max-sm:hidden">View Full Service Area</span>
        </Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Container>
</section>

<style>
  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     MAP PANEL
     A gradient-washed surface with a hairline border and a soft, wide
     shadow - the container is meant to read as a premium card holding the
     map, not as a plain bounding box.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  .map-panel {
    /* Capped and centred: the SVG's aspect ratio is ~1.58:1, so an
       unbounded panel on a 1440+ screen made the map ~710px tall and it
       dominated the page. Constraining the panel is the right lever -
       capping the SVG's height instead silently caps its width too, and
       the map stops filling the panel. */
    max-width: 62rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    border-radius: 2rem;
    padding: 0.75rem;
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-teal-900) 10%, transparent);
    background:
      radial-gradient(120% 90% at 18% 8%, color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-pink-350) 9%, transparent), transparent 55%),
      linear-gradient(165deg, var(--color-teal-50) 0%, #ffffff 48%, var(--color-teal-50) 100%);
    box-shadow:
      0 1px 2px rgba(0, 40, 52, 0.03),
      0 18px 48px -18px rgba(0, 40, 52, 0.22);
  }
  @media (min-width: 640px) {
    .map-panel {
      padding: 1.75rem 2rem;
    }
  }

  /* No max-height on the SVG: it has a fixed aspect ratio, so capping the
     height silently caps the WIDTH too and the map stops filling the
     panel. The panel's own max-width controls the overall scale instead. */
  .map-svg {
    display: block;
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     PUGET SOUND INSET
     Sits to the right of the national map on desktop as a self-contained
     card. The two maps use DIFFERENT projections on purpose - the US map
     is geoAlbersUsa (correct for the whole country), the inset a
     geoMercator fitted to the service-area bbox (correct at metro scale).
     Sharing one projection is what made all 48 cities overlap.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  /* The inset carries the location count from 1024px up, so the on-map
     sub-label would just be a duplicate. */
  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .map-sublabel {
      display: none;
    }
  }

  @media (min-width: 1024px) {
    .map-panel {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) 18.5rem;
      gap: 1.5rem;
      align-items: center;
      max-width: 78rem;
    }
  }
  .map-inset {
    border-radius: 1.25rem;
    padding: 0.75rem 0.75rem 0.9rem;
    background: color-mix(in oklab, white 78%, transparent);
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-teal-900) 9%, transparent);
    box-shadow: 0 8px 26px -12px rgba(0, 40, 52, 0.28);
  }
  .inset-svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .inset-wa {
    fill: var(--color-teal-100);
    fill-opacity: 0.5;
    stroke: var(--color-teal-400);
    stroke-opacity: 0.4;
    stroke-width: 1;
  }
  .inset-county {
    fill: var(--color-pink-350);
    fill-opacity: 0.24;
    stroke: var(--color-pink-600);
    stroke-opacity: 0.45;
    stroke-width: 1.2;
  }
  .inset-dot {
    fill: var(--color-pink-600);
    stroke: white;
    stroke-width: 1;
  }
  .inset-dot-hero {
    fill: var(--color-pink-500);
    stroke: var(--color-teal-950);
    stroke-width: 2;
  }
  .inset-pulse {
    fill-opacity: 0.22;
  }
  .inset-label {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    fill: var(--color-teal-900);
    fill-opacity: 0.82;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .inset-caption {
    margin-top: 0.4rem;
    text-align: center;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .inset-caption-title {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--color-teal-950);
  }
  .inset-caption-sub {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    color: var(--color-ink-500);
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     STATES
     Each state is an individually filled path. The separator stroke is the
     panel's own light tint rather than a dark line, so the states read as
     butted tiles and the country's silhouette stays crisp.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     LABEL HIERARCHY
     Three deliberately separated tiers, so the eye reads "country for
     context, Pinky's operates HERE":
       1. state abbreviations - teal-900, 70% opacity
       2. reference cities    - teal-900, 50%, tiny, small teal dot
       3. service area        - brand pink, full contrast, pin + pulse
     Tier 3 is roughly 2.5x the type size of tier 2 and the only thing on
     the map using pink, which is what keeps it dominant.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  .map-state-label {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 16px;
    font-weight: 700;
    fill: var(--color-teal-900);
    fill-opacity: 0.7;
    letter-spacing: 0.02em;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  .map-leader {
    stroke: var(--color-teal-900);
    stroke-opacity: 0.35;
    stroke-width: 1;
  }
  .map-callout-leader {
    stroke: var(--color-pink-600);
    stroke-opacity: 0.5;
    stroke-width: 1.6;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }
  .map-ref-dot {
    fill: var(--color-teal-800);
    fill-opacity: 0.55;
  }
  .map-ref-label {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 11.5px;
    font-weight: 600;
    fill: var(--color-teal-900);
    fill-opacity: 0.5;
    pointer-events: none;
  }
  /* Real Pinky's locations - pink, never teal, so they can never be
     confused with the reference cities above. */
  .map-svc-dot {
    fill: var(--color-pink-600);
    stroke: white;
    stroke-width: 1.1;
  }

  .map-state {
    fill-opacity: 0.34;
    stroke: color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-teal-50) 80%, white);
    stroke-width: 1.5;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    transition:
      fill-opacity 0.28s ease,
      stroke 0.28s ease;
  }
  /* Pointer-only, so it never sticks as a phantom :hover after a tap. */
  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .map-state:hover {
      fill-opacity: 0.58;
      stroke: white;
    }
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     WASHINGTON - the hero state
  ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  .map-state-active {
    stroke: var(--color-teal-900);
    stroke-width: 2;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    filter: drop-shadow(0 4px 14px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-pink-500) 55%, transparent));
    transition:
      filter 0.3s ease,
      transform 0.3s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
    transform-box: fill-box;
    transform-origin: center;
  }
  .map-wa-link {
    cursor: pointer;
  }

  /* ---- TEXAS ----
     Level 1 in the hierarchy alongside Washington, but visually quieter:
     a tint rather than a solid fill, and no glow. That difference is
     load-bearing - it is what stops the map reading as "all of Texas is
     served" when only two cities are confirmed. */
  .map-state-tx {
    fill-opacity: 0.3;
    stroke: var(--color-pink-600);
    stroke-opacity: 0.85;
    stroke-width: 1.8;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    transition:
      fill-opacity 0.28s ease,
      stroke-opacity 0.28s ease;
  }
  .map-tx-link {
    cursor: pointer;
  }
  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .map-tx-link:hover .map-state-tx {
      fill-opacity: 0.44;
    }
  }
  .map-tx-link:focus-visible {
    outline: none;
  }
  .map-tx-link:focus-visible .map-state-tx {
    stroke: var(--color-pink-600);
    stroke-width: 4;
    fill-opacity: 0.44;
  }
  .map-tooltip-tx {
    transform: translate(300px, 330px) scale(0.96);
    transform-origin: 300px 330px;
  }
  .map-tx-link:focus-visible .map-tooltip-tx {
    transform: translate(300px, 330px) scale(1);
  }
  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .map-tx-link:hover .map-tooltip-tx {
      transform: translate(300px, 330px) scale(1);
    }
  }

  /* Texas service markers - same pink language as Washington's dots, and
     deliberately unlike the muted teal reference dots (Dallas, Houston). */
  .map-tx-dot {
    fill: var(--color-pink-600);
    stroke: white;
    stroke-width: 1.6;
  }
  .map-tx-pulse {
    fill-opacity: 0.22;
  }
  .map-tx-label {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-size: 13px;
    font-weight: 700;
    fill: var(--color-teal-950);
    pointer-events: none;
    paint-order: stroke;
    stroke: white;
    stroke-width: 3.5;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  /* Tooltip: hidden until the state is hovered or keyboard-focused.
     pointer-events:none so it can never swallow the click meant for the
     link underneath it. Visibility (not just opacity) is toggled so it is
     genuinely inert when hidden. */
  .map-tooltip {
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    transform: translate(196px, 26px) scale(0.96);
    transform-origin: 196px 26px;
    transition:
      opacity 0.22s ease,
      transform 0.22s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1),
      visibility 0.22s;
    filter: drop-shadow(0 8px 22px rgba(0, 40, 52, 0.32));
  }
  .map-wa-link:focus-visible .map-tooltip {
    opacity: 1;
    visibility: visible;
    transform: translate(196px, 26px) scale(1);
  }
  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .map-wa-link:hover .map-tooltip {
      opacity: 1;
      visibility: visible;
      transform: translate(196px, 26px) scale(1);
    }
  }
  /* Phones: no hover, and at ~260px wide the tooltip would cover half the
     country. The heading and city list below carry the same information. */
  @media (max-width: 639px) {
    .map-tooltip {
      display: none;
    }
  }
  @media (hover: hover) and (pointer: fine) {
    .map-wa-link:hover .map-state-active {
      filter: drop-shadow(0 6px 20px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-pink-500) 75%, transparent));
      transform: scale(1.04);
    }
  }
  /* Visible keyboard focus: an outline on the SVG <a> itself would be
     clipped, so the ring is drawn on the state path instead. */
  .map-wa-link:focus-visible {
    outline: none;
  }
  .map-wa-link:focus-visible .map-state-active {
    stroke: var(--color-pink-600);
    stroke-width: 4;
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 0 3px white)
      drop-shadow(0 6px 20px color-mix(in oklab, var(--color-pink-500) 75%, transparent));
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     SEATTLE MARKER
     Animating the circle's `r` rather than a CSS transform: a transform on
     an SVG child resolves against its own origin and fights the parent
     <g>'s translate, which detached the ripple from the pin.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  .map-pulse {
    fill-opacity: 0.25;
    animation: map-pulse 3s cubic-bezier(0.4, 0, 0.6, 1) infinite;
  }
  .map-pulse-delay {
    animation-delay: 1.5s;
  }
  @keyframes map-pulse {
    0% {
      r: 5;
      fill-opacity: 0.32;
    }
    70%,
    100% {
      r: 30;
      fill-opacity: 0;
    }
  }

  /* ------------------------------------------------------------------
     ENTRANCE
     Staggered: country fades up, Washington's highlight blooms, then the
     pin drops in. Driven by the same [data-reveal] IntersectionObserver
     the rest of the site uses (the panel carries the attribute), so there
     is no extra script and no animation library.
  ------------------------------------------------------------------ */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    html.js.io-ready [data-reveal] .map-states {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: scale(0.985);
      transform-origin: center;
      transition:
        opacity 0.7s ease,
        transform 0.7s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
    }
    html.js.io-ready [data-reveal].is-revealed .map-states {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: scale(1);
    }
    html.js.io-ready [data-reveal] .map-state-active {
      opacity: 0;
      transition:
        opacity 0.5s ease 0.35s,
        filter 0.3s ease,
        transform 0.3s cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1);
    }
    html.js.io-ready [data-reveal].is-revealed .map-state-active {
      opacity: 1;
    }
    html.js.io-ready [data-reveal] .map-marker,
    html.js.io-ready [data-reveal] .map-label,
    html.js.io-ready [data-reveal] .map-sublabel {
      opacity: 0;
      transition: opacity 0.5s ease 0.6s;
    }
    html.js.io-ready [data-reveal].is-revealed .map-marker,
    html.js.io-ready [data-reveal].is-revealed .map-label,
    html.js.io-ready [data-reveal].is-revealed .map-sublabel {
      opacity: 1;
    }
